/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-103 Operating System Security (Windows & Linux) */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-103"] = {
ov:"Endpoints are where most compromises land and where most defensive value is won or lost. This course teaches the security models of Windows and Linux from the kernel outward, then the concrete hardening, logging, and auditing practices that make a host defensible. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios, so treat the configuration detail as testable.",
mods:[
{t:"OS Security Architecture: Kernel and User Space",d:"Privilege rings, system calls, and why the boundary matters.",
obj:["Explain the separation between kernel space and user space","Describe how system calls mediate privileged operations","Explain why kernel-level compromise is catastrophic"],
read:["Operating systems enforce security by separating privileged kernel space from unprivileged user space. The kernel manages hardware, memory, and processes with full privilege; user applications run with restricted privilege and must request privileged operations through system calls — a controlled gateway where the kernel validates the request before acting. This ring model (rooted in CPU protection rings) is the foundation of every OS security control: a user process cannot directly touch another process's memory or the hardware without the kernel's mediation.",
"The boundary is why privilege matters so much. Code running in kernel space — a driver, a rootkit, a kernel exploit — has unrestricted access to everything: it can hide processes, tamper with security tools, and read all memory. This is why kernel-mode rootkits are the hardest malware to detect and remove, and why exploits that achieve kernel execution are rated most severe. Conversely, keeping code in user space and minimizing what runs privileged is a core defensive strategy — the least-privilege principle applied at the OS level.",
"Modern systems add layers: virtualization-based security isolates critical kernel components even from the rest of the kernel, and hardware features (SMEP, SMAP, secure enclaves) harden the boundary further. As a defender you do not need to write kernel code, but you must understand that a request to run something as SYSTEM (Windows) or root (Linux) is a request to operate near this boundary, and that the most dangerous compromises are those that cross into privileged execution. Every hardening decision in this course ultimately protects this line."],
ex:"A malware sample requests a kernel driver load to install a rootkit. Because Windows requires drivers to be signed and, with HVCI enabled, validated by virtualization-based security, the unsigned driver load fails and the rootkit cannot reach kernel space — the malware is confined to user space where EDR can still see and stop it. The defense worked by protecting the kernel boundary.",
refs:[["Windows Internals, Part 1, Ch.1-2","Architecture of Windows: kernel/user mode, processes, and system service dispatch."],["The Linux Programming Interface, Ch.3","System calls and the user/kernel boundary in Linux."]],
kc:[
{q:"A user-space application that needs to read a file must:",o:["Access the disk hardware directly","Make a system call so the kernel validates and performs the privileged operation","Switch the CPU to a lower privilege ring","Load a kernel driver"],a:1,x:"User processes cannot touch hardware directly; system calls are the mediated gateway where the kernel checks and performs privileged actions."},
{q:"Why is kernel-mode malware (a rootkit) so dangerous?",o:["It runs slower and is easy to spot","It has unrestricted access to all memory and hardware and can hide from and disable security tools","It cannot persist","It only affects user files"],a:1,x:"Kernel privilege means the malware can subvert the very mechanisms meant to detect it, making it the hardest class to find and remove."},
{q:"Requiring driver signing and validation (e.g., HVCI) primarily protects:",o:["User document files","The kernel boundary, by preventing unsigned/untrusted code from executing in kernel space","Network bandwidth","The display resolution"],a:1,x:"Driver-signing enforcement keeps untrusted code out of kernel mode, defending the most critical privilege boundary in the OS."}]},
{t:"The Windows Security Model",d:"SIDs, access tokens, ACLs, and privileges.",
obj:["Explain SIDs, access tokens, and security descriptors","Describe how DACLs mediate object access","Explain user rights and privileges versus permissions"],
read:["Windows mediates access through a consistent model. Every security principal (user, group, computer) has a Security Identifier (SID). When a user logs on, Windows builds an access token containing their SID, group SIDs, and privileges; this token accompanies every process they run. Securable objects (files, registry keys, services) carry a security descriptor with a Discretionary Access Control List (DACL) — an ordered list of access control entries granting or denying specific rights to specific SIDs. On each access, Windows compares the token's SIDs against the DACL to decide.",
"A crucial distinction is permissions versus privileges (user rights). Permissions are object-specific (this user can read this file), enforced by DACLs. Privileges are system-wide capabilities held in the token (SeDebugPrivilege to debug any process, SeBackupPrivilege to bypass file permissions for backup, SeImpersonatePrivilege to act as another user). Attackers hunt privileges because a single powerful privilege can bypass many permissions — SeBackupPrivilege reads any file regardless of its DACL, and SeImpersonatePrivilege underlies many privilege-escalation exploits.",
"Understanding this model turns abstract hardening into concrete decisions: minimizing membership in privileged groups (Administrators, Backup Operators) shrinks who holds dangerous privileges; auditing DACLs on sensitive objects controls permissions; and monitoring for anomalous privilege use (a normal user suddenly wielding SeDebugPrivilege) detects escalation. The credential and token model here is also the substrate for the identity attacks (pass-the-hash, token theft) you will study in later courses — you cannot defend against them without understanding tokens."],
ex:"A service account is granted membership in Backup Operators for a backup task. An attacker who compromises that account discovers it holds SeBackupPrivilege and reads the domain controller's NTDS.dit — the entire password database — bypassing every file permission. The over-privileged group membership, not a software flaw, was the vulnerability; least-privilege scoping of the service account would have prevented it.",
refs:[["Windows Internals, Part 1, Ch.7","Security: SIDs, tokens, access checks, and privileges."],["Microsoft, Access Control Lists documentation","How DACLs and security descriptors govern object access."]],
kc:[
{q:"When a Windows user logs on, the access token attached to their processes contains:",o:["Only their password hash","Their SID, group SIDs, and privileges, used for every access check","The full DACL of every file","Their MAC address"],a:1,x:"The token carries the user's identity and capabilities; Windows checks it against each object's DACL on access."},
{q:"A privilege such as SeBackupPrivilege is dangerous because it:",o:["Only allows reading the user's own files","Can bypass normal file permissions system-wide, reading any file regardless of its DACL","Encrypts the disk","Disables the network"],a:1,x:"Privileges are system-wide capabilities; SeBackupPrivilege overrides DACLs, letting the holder read sensitive files like the credential store."},
{q:"The most effective structural defense against privilege abuse is:",o:["Longer passwords only","Minimizing membership in privileged groups so few tokens hold dangerous privileges","Disabling the GUI","Renaming the Administrator account alone"],a:1,x:"Since privileges ride in tokens granted by group membership, shrinking privileged group membership directly reduces the attack surface."}]},
{t:"Windows Hardening and Group Policy",d:"Baselines, GPOs, and reducing the attack surface at scale.",
obj:["Explain how Group Policy enforces configuration at scale","Identify high-value Windows hardening settings","Describe security baselines and their sources"],
read:["Group Policy is how Windows configuration is enforced consistently across thousands of machines: policies defined centrally are applied to organizational units, controlling everything from password rules and audit settings to which scripts run and which features are disabled. Manual per-machine hardening does not scale and drifts; Group Policy (and its modern cloud counterparts) makes a hardened state uniform and self-healing, reapplying settings that local users or malware alter. This centralization is powerful and, if the domain is compromised, dangerous — which is why domain controllers are the crown jewels.",
"High-value hardening reduces attack surface and closes well-known abuse paths: disabling legacy protocols (SMBv1, NTLMv1) that enable relay and worm attacks, restricting PowerShell with Constrained Language Mode and logging, disabling Office macros from the internet, enforcing LSASS protection to hamper credential theft, removing local administrator rights from users, and disabling unused services and features. Each setting maps to a specific attacker technique; hardening is not generic tightening but deliberate removal of the paths attackers actually use.",
"Rather than inventing settings, defenders start from published security baselines — the Microsoft Security Compliance Toolkit, CIS Benchmarks (module thirteen), and DISA STIGs — which encode expert-vetted configurations. These provide a defensible starting point and an audit standard: deviations become documented risk decisions rather than accidents. The discipline is to apply a baseline, measure compliance, document justified exceptions, and monitor for drift — the same governance pattern from BC-101 applied to endpoint configuration."],
ex:"A ransomware worm spreads via SMBv1. Organizations that had disabled SMBv1 through Group Policy (a single baseline setting) were immune to the network-propagation vector, while those that left the legacy protocol enabled watched it spread machine to machine. One centrally-enforced hardening setting was the difference between a contained infection and an enterprise-wide outbreak.",
refs:[["Microsoft Security Compliance Toolkit","Official security baselines and the tools to assess against them."],["DISA Windows STIGs","Defense-grade configuration standards for Windows systems."]],
kc:[
{q:"Group Policy's main security advantage over manual hardening is that it:",o:["Runs faster","Enforces configuration consistently at scale and reapplies settings that drift or are tampered with","Requires no domain","Encrypts Group Policy files"],a:1,x:"Centralized, self-healing enforcement keeps thousands of machines in a uniform hardened state, which manual configuration cannot achieve."},
{q:"Disabling SMBv1 across an enterprise primarily prevents:",o:["Slow file transfers","Worm-style lateral propagation and relay attacks that abuse the legacy protocol","User login","Printing"],a:1,x:"SMBv1 enabled famous worm propagation; removing it closes that specific network-spread vector."},
{q:"Security baselines like CIS Benchmarks and DISA STIGs provide:",o:["Antivirus signatures","Expert-vetted configuration standards that serve as both a hardening starting point and an audit reference","Network diagrams","Password managers"],a:1,x:"Baselines encode vetted settings; applying one and measuring compliance turns hardening into a documented, auditable process."}]},
{t:"Windows Event Logging and Auditing",d:"What to log, which Event IDs matter, and central collection.",
obj:["Describe the Windows event log structure and key channels","Identify high-value security Event IDs for detection","Explain the need for central log collection and tamper resistance"],
read:["Windows records security-relevant activity in event logs, and configured well they are a defender's richest endpoint telemetry. The Security channel captures logon events, privilege use, object access, and account changes; the System and Application channels capture operational events; and specialized channels (PowerShell, Sysmon if installed, Windows Defender) capture deep behavioral detail. Crucially, most valuable auditing is off by default — audit policy must be deliberately enabled (via Group Policy) to log process creation, logon types, and object access at useful granularity.",
"Certain Event IDs are the backbone of endpoint detection: 4624/4625 (successful/failed logon, with logon type revealing remote vs. interactive vs. network), 4672 (special privileges assigned — a signal of admin logon), 4688 (process creation, ideally with command lines enabled), 4720/4732 (account creation, group membership changes), 7045 (service installation — a common persistence method), and 1102 (audit log cleared — itself a strong compromise indicator). Sysmon dramatically enriches this with process lineage, network connections, and hashes. Knowing these IDs lets you turn raw logs into detections.",
"Logs on the endpoint are within an attacker's reach, so central collection is essential: forwarding events to a SIEM or log collector (Windows Event Forwarding) puts them beyond the attacker's ability to erase and enables correlation across hosts. An attacker who clears local logs (Event ID 1102) cannot unclear the forwarded copy, and the clearing event itself becomes a detection. This is the endpoint feed into the monitoring and hunting disciplines taught in the operational tier."],
ex:"During an investigation, local Security logs on a compromised host are suspiciously short — Event ID 1102 shows they were cleared. But Windows Event Forwarding had already shipped the events to the SIEM, where 4688 process-creation logs reveal the attacker's PowerShell commands and a 7045 shows their persistence service. Central collection defeated the log-clearing, and the clearing itself was a flag.",
refs:[["Microsoft, Windows Security audit events reference","The catalog of Security Event IDs and what each records."],["NSA/CISA, Windows Event Forwarding guidance","Configuring central, tamper-resistant event collection and high-value auditing."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why must Windows audit policy be explicitly configured for effective detection?",o:["Logging is illegal by default","Much high-value auditing (process creation, detailed logon) is off by default and must be enabled via policy","Windows never logs anything","It only affects performance"],a:1,x:"Default logging is minimal; deliberate audit-policy configuration (often plus Sysmon) is required to capture detection-grade detail."},
{q:"Event ID 1102 (the security log was cleared) is significant because it:",o:["Indicates a normal reboot","Is a strong indicator of attacker anti-forensics — someone erased evidence","Means logging is disabled permanently","Is generated hourly"],a:1,x:"Legitimate operations rarely clear the security log; a 1102 event is a high-signal indicator of an attacker covering tracks."},
{q:"Forwarding events to a central collector primarily defeats which attacker action?",o:["Password guessing","Local log deletion — the forwarded copy survives even if the endpoint logs are cleared","Network scanning","Phishing"],a:1,x:"Central collection puts logs beyond the endpoint's reach, so clearing local logs no longer destroys the evidence."}]},
{t:"Linux File Permissions and Ownership",d:"The rwx model, special bits, and safe defaults.",
obj:["Read and set standard rwx permissions and ownership","Explain setuid, setgid, and the sticky bit and their risks","Describe umask and safe default permissions"],
read:["Linux access control starts with the classic model: every file has an owner, a group, and three permission triads — read, write, execute — for owner, group, and others, displayed as strings like rwxr-x---. Reading these fluently is basic literacy: rwxr-x--- means the owner has full access, the group can read and execute, and others have nothing. Directories reinterpret the bits (execute means the ability to traverse into the directory, read means listing its contents), a common source of confusion and misconfiguration.",
"Special permission bits carry outsized security weight. Setuid on an executable makes it run with the file owner's privileges rather than the caller's — essential for tools like passwd but dangerous, because a setuid-root binary with a vulnerability hands the attacker root. Setgid behaves similarly for groups, and on directories forces new files to inherit the directory's group. The sticky bit on a shared directory (like /tmp) prevents users from deleting each other's files. Auditing for unexpected setuid-root binaries is a standard hardening and incident-response step precisely because they are a favorite privilege-escalation path.",
"Default permissions are governed by umask, which subtracts permissions from newly created files — a umask of 022 yields files that others can read, while 027 or 077 restricts more tightly. Safe defaults matter because a permissive umask silently creates world-readable sensitive files across a system. The permission model is simple but its edges — directory semantics, special bits, defaults — are where real vulnerabilities live, which is why the knowledge check probes them rather than the obvious cases."],
ex:"An incident responder runs a find for setuid-root binaries and discovers a copy of a shell (/usr/local/bin/.sh) owned by root with the setuid bit set — a backdoor left by an attacker so any user can spawn a root shell. Standard permissions look normal; only auditing the special bits revealed the persistence mechanism.",
refs:[["The Linux Programming Interface, Ch.15","File permissions, ownership, and the setuid/setgid/sticky semantics."],["CIS Linux Benchmark, filesystem section","Auditing setuid binaries, umask, and permission hardening."]],
kc:[
{q:"The permission string rwxr-x--- grants the group:",o:["Read, write, and execute","Read and execute only","No access","Write only"],a:1,x:"The middle triad r-x means read and execute for the group; the owner has rwx and others have nothing."},
{q:"A setuid-root binary is a security concern because:",o:["It runs slower","A vulnerability in it lets a normal user execute code with root privileges","It cannot be deleted","It disables logging"],a:1,x:"Setuid makes the program run as its owner (root); exploiting such a binary is a classic local privilege-escalation path."},
{q:"On a directory, the execute (x) permission controls:",o:["Whether files inside can be run","The ability to traverse into (cd) and access files within the directory","Encryption of the directory","Network sharing"],a:1,x:"For directories, execute means traversal — without it you cannot enter the directory even if you can read its listing."}]},
{t:"Linux Users, sudo, and Privilege",d:"Root, least privilege, and controlled escalation.",
obj:["Explain the root account and the risks of direct root use","Describe how sudo provides least-privilege escalation and accountability","Identify common sudo misconfigurations"],
read:["Root (UID 0) holds unrestricted power, and the mature discipline is to never log in directly as root but to escalate specific commands through sudo. Sudo grants named users the ability to run specific commands as root (or another user), logging each invocation — providing both least privilege (a user gets only the commands they need) and accountability (the log shows who ran what as whom). Shared root passwords destroy accountability; sudo restores it while shrinking the standing privilege each administrator holds.",
"Sudo's power makes its configuration a high-value target. The /etc/sudoers file (edited with visudo) defines who may run what; misconfigurations are a leading local-privilege-escalation vector. Granting a user sudo access to a program that can spawn a shell or write arbitrary files (vi, less, find, many interpreters) effectively grants full root, because the user escapes the intended command into a root shell. NOPASSWD entries and overly broad ALL=(ALL) grants similarly widen the blast radius. Auditing sudoers against a least-privilege standard is essential hardening.",
"The principle generalizes: standing privilege is risk, and just-in-time, logged, narrowly-scoped escalation is the goal. This mirrors the Windows least-privilege lessons and foreshadows privileged access management. An attacker who compromises a normal account gains little; one who finds a sudo misconfiguration or an over-privileged account gains everything — so the defensive value of tight sudo policy is enormous relative to its cost."],
ex:"A sudoers entry grants a developer NOPASSWD access to run vi as root for editing a config. The attacker who phishes that developer runs sudo vi, then uses vi's shell-escape (:!sh) to spawn a root shell — full compromise from a single overly-generous line. Restricting the grant to a purpose-built, non-escaping editor or a specific tightly-scoped command would have prevented the escalation.",
refs:[["sudoers manual (man 5 sudoers)","How sudo rules are defined and the security implications of each option."],["GTFOBins project","Documented shell-escape techniques showing why sudo access to certain binaries equals root."]],
kc:[
{q:"Sudo is preferred over shared root login primarily because it provides:",o:["Faster command execution","Least-privilege, per-command escalation with per-user logging and accountability","Encryption of commands","Automatic patching"],a:1,x:"Sudo grants only needed commands and logs who ran what, restoring accountability that shared root credentials destroy."},
{q:"Granting sudo access to an editor like vi effectively grants root because:",o:["Editors are always malicious","The editor can spawn a shell (shell escape), letting the user break out into a root command line","vi encrypts files","It changes the umask"],a:1,x:"Programs that can launch a subshell let the user escape the intended command into a full root shell — a classic escalation."},
{q:"A NOPASSWD ALL=(ALL) sudo grant to a normal user is dangerous because it:",o:["Slows the system","Effectively gives that account unrestricted root with no reauthentication — a total escalation if the account is compromised","Only affects that user's files","Disables sudo logging"],a:1,x:"Broad NOPASSWD grants remove both the privilege scoping and the password barrier, so compromising the account equals compromising root."}]},
{t:"SSH Hardening",d:"Key-based auth, configuration, and defending the primary admin path.",
obj:["Explain why key-based authentication beats passwords for SSH","Identify key sshd_config hardening options","Describe defenses against SSH brute force and abuse"],
read:["SSH is the primary remote-administration path for Linux, which makes it a primary target — internet-exposed SSH is scanned and brute-forced continuously. The foundational hardening is to disable password authentication entirely and require key-based authentication: a cryptographic key pair resists guessing in a way passwords cannot, and combined with a passphrase-protected private key it provides strong two-factor-like assurance. Disabling direct root login (PermitRootLogin no) forces attackers to compromise a named account and then escalate, adding a barrier and an audit trail.",
"The sshd_config file holds the hardening levers: PasswordAuthentication no, PermitRootLogin no, restricting AllowUsers/AllowGroups to only those who need access, choosing strong modern ciphers and key-exchange algorithms while disabling legacy ones, setting idle timeouts, and limiting authentication attempts. Where possible, SSH should not be exposed directly to the internet at all but reached through a bastion host or VPN, shrinking the attack surface to a single hardened, heavily-monitored entry point.",
"Brute-force and credential attacks are met with layered defenses: fail2ban or equivalent to block IPs after repeated failures, network-level restriction of source addresses, and — critically — monitoring of authentication logs for successful logins from unusual sources or at unusual times, because a successful brute force looks like a normal login unless you baseline. SSH keys themselves must be managed: orphaned authorized_keys entries are a common persistence and backdoor mechanism attackers add, so auditing them is part of both hardening and incident response."],
ex:"An attacker adds their own public key to a service account's authorized_keys file after an initial compromise, granting themselves passwordless persistent access that survives password resets. Routine SSH hardening audits that review authorized_keys files against an approved inventory catch this backdoor — a persistence technique invisible to password-focused defenses.",
refs:[["OpenSSH sshd_config manual","The full set of server hardening options and their meanings."],["CIS Linux Benchmark, SSH section","Vetted SSH server configuration for defense-grade hardening."]],
kc:[
{q:"Disabling password authentication in favor of SSH keys primarily defeats:",o:["Man-in-the-middle attacks","Password brute-force and credential-guessing attacks that continuously target exposed SSH","Disk failure","DNS spoofing"],a:1,x:"Cryptographic keys cannot be guessed like passwords, neutralizing the automated brute-forcing that exposed SSH constantly faces."},
{q:"Setting PermitRootLogin no forces an attacker to:",o:["Give up entirely","Compromise a named user account and then escalate, adding a barrier and an audit trail","Use a different port","Encrypt their traffic"],a:1,x:"Blocking direct root login means the attacker must first take a named account (logged) and then escalate, rather than going straight to root."},
{q:"An unexpected entry in a user's authorized_keys file most likely indicates:",o:["A normal configuration","An attacker-planted backdoor for passwordless persistent access","A software update","A DNS record"],a:1,x:"Attackers add their own public keys to maintain access that survives password changes; auditing authorized_keys detects this persistence."}]},
{t:"Linux Logging: syslog, journald, auditd",d:"Where Linux records activity and how to make it useful.",
obj:["Describe the roles of syslog, journald, and auditd","Identify high-value Linux log sources for detection","Explain central forwarding and log integrity"],
read:["Linux logging spans several systems. Traditional syslog (and rsyslog/syslog-ng) writes text logs to /var/log for authentication (auth.log/secure), the kernel, and applications. Systemd's journald provides a structured, queryable journal. The Linux Audit system (auditd) is the deepest source: a kernel-level framework that can record system calls, file access, and command execution against configurable rules, producing the granular detail needed to reconstruct attacker actions. Each layer serves a purpose, and detection-grade visibility usually requires auditd rules deliberately configured, much as Windows auditing must be enabled.",
"High-value sources map to attacker behavior: auth logs reveal logins, sudo use, and SSH activity; auditd can capture execve calls (every command run, with arguments) and writes to sensitive files; and application logs (web servers, databases) reveal exploitation attempts. Configuring auditd to watch identity files (/etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/sudoers), authentication events, and command execution turns a quiet host into a rich sensor. Without this configuration, a compromised Linux host often yields far less forensic detail than a comparably-configured Windows host.",
"As on Windows, local logs are attacker-reachable, so forwarding to a central collector is essential for both tamper resistance and cross-host correlation. Attackers routinely clear or truncate /var/log files; forwarded copies survive, and gaps or truncation in local logs become indicators themselves. Time synchronization via NTP across hosts is again non-negotiable, since correlating auth events across systems with skewed clocks produces false timelines. This host telemetry is the Linux feed into the SIEM and hunting practices of the operational tier."],
ex:"After a web-server compromise, local /var/log/auth.log has been truncated to hide the attacker's sudo escalation. But auditd rules had logged the execve of every command the attacker ran, and those records were forwarded to the SIEM before truncation — reconstructing the full sequence from initial shell to root. Deliberate auditd configuration plus forwarding preserved the evidence the attacker tried to destroy.",
refs:[["Red Hat, Understanding the Linux Audit system","Configuring auditd rules for security-relevant events."],["NIST SP 800-92","Log management — sources, central collection, retention, and integrity."]],
kc:[
{q:"The Linux subsystem best suited to recording every command executed with arguments is:",o:["syslog by default","auditd, when configured with execve rules","The DHCP client","The kernel ring buffer only"],a:1,x:"auditd is a kernel-level audit framework that, with appropriate rules, captures system calls like execve — the granular command-execution detail syslog does not provide by default."},
{q:"Watching /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, and /etc/sudoers with auditd rules helps detect:",o:["Network latency","Unauthorized changes to accounts, credentials, and privilege configuration","Disk fragmentation","Display errors"],a:1,x:"These files govern identity and privilege; auditing writes to them catches account creation and privilege-escalation tampering."},
{q:"Truncated or missing /var/log entries on a compromised host should be treated as:",o:["A normal cleanup","A potential anti-forensics indicator; forwarded central copies are needed to recover the evidence","Proof nothing happened","A logging bug to ignore"],a:1,x:"Attackers clear local logs; gaps are themselves suspicious, and central forwarding preserves the record beyond the attacker's reach."}]},
{t:"Service and Process Management",d:"Attack surface, daemons, and least functionality.",
obj:["Explain how running services define attack surface","Describe least-functionality hardening","Identify persistence via services and scheduled tasks"],
read:["Every listening service is a potential entry point, so the least-functionality principle — run only what is needed — is among the highest-yield hardening practices. A default OS install often enables services that a given host will never use; each is code that can be exploited, misconfigured, or abused. Enumerating listening services (ss/netstat, systemctl on Linux; services.msc and Get-Service on Windows) and disabling the unnecessary ones directly shrinks the attack surface, and doing so from a baseline (module three, module thirteen) makes it repeatable rather than ad hoc.",
"Services and scheduled tasks are also prime persistence mechanisms. Attackers install malicious services (Windows Event ID 7045 flags service creation) or cron jobs / systemd timers (Linux) so their code survives reboots and re-launches automatically. Defenders therefore inventory not just what should run but establish a baseline of legitimate services and scheduled tasks, so that a new, unexpected service — often with a random name or an unusual binary path — stands out. This is the endpoint analog of the network beaconing detection from BC-102.",
"Process monitoring extends this at runtime: unusual parent-child relationships (a Word document spawning PowerShell, a web server spawning a shell), processes running from temporary directories, and unsigned binaries in system paths are behavioral indicators that EDR and Sysmon surface. The combination — minimize what runs, baseline what should run, and monitor how processes behave — is the practical core of endpoint defense, and it depends on understanding the service and process model this module builds."],
ex:"A baseline of a web server's services is captured at hardening time. Weeks later, monitoring flags a new systemd service named 'sysupdate' running a binary from /tmp — not in the baseline. Investigation confirms attacker persistence installed after a web exploit. The deviation from the known-good service inventory, not any signature, caught it.",
refs:[["CIS Benchmarks, services sections (Windows/Linux)","Which services to disable and how to enforce least functionality."],["MITRE ATT&CK, Persistence (TA0003)","Service creation, scheduled task, and cron-based persistence techniques."]],
kc:[
{q:"The least-functionality principle reduces risk by:",o:["Encrypting all services","Running only necessary services, so there is less exposed, exploitable, or abusable code","Increasing the number of daemons","Disabling logging"],a:1,x:"Each running service is attack surface; disabling the unnecessary ones directly shrinks what an attacker can target."},
{q:"A newly created service with a random name running a binary from a temp directory most likely indicates:",o:["A Windows update","Attacker persistence installed to survive reboots","A DNS cache refresh","Normal load balancing"],a:1,x:"Malicious services from unusual paths are a classic persistence mechanism; deviation from the service baseline surfaces them."},
{q:"A Word document process spawning PowerShell is significant because it is:",o:["Normal Office behavior to ignore","An anomalous parent-child relationship indicating macro-based code execution","A hardware fault","A network timeout"],a:1,x:"Office apps do not normally launch scripting engines; this process lineage is a strong behavioral indicator of malicious macro execution."}]},
{t:"OS Patch and Update Management",d:"Timely patching, testing, and legacy exceptions at the host level.",
obj:["Explain why OS patch latency is a core risk metric","Describe staged patching and rollback","Handle unpatchable legacy systems with compensating controls"],
read:["Unpatched operating systems are among the most reliably exploited targets, because published vulnerabilities come with public exploits and automated scanning finds vulnerable hosts within hours. Patch latency — the time from a fix being available to it being deployed on exposed systems — is therefore a first-order risk metric (introduced in BC-101's measurement module). The goal is not instant patching everywhere, which risks stability, but a disciplined process that patches internet-facing and critical systems fast and everything else on a defensible cadence.",
"Responsible patching is staged to balance security against availability: patches are tested in a representative environment or on a pilot ring, deployed in waves with monitoring, and backed by a rollback plan for the inevitable bad update. This staging is why patch latency is measured rather than assumed zero — the process takes real time, and pretending otherwise leads either to reckless deployment that breaks production or to paralysis that leaves systems exposed. Severity-based SLAs (critical internet-facing within days, others within weeks) make the trade-off explicit.",
"Some systems cannot be patched promptly — vendor-locked appliances, legacy applications certified against a specific OS version, operational technology on availability constraints. These are not exceptions to be ignored but risks to be managed with compensating controls (BC-101): isolation and segmentation so the unpatched host is unreachable from likely attack paths, enhanced monitoring, and documented risk acceptance with an owner and a review date. The unpatchable machine is defensible; the unpatchable-and-forgotten machine is a breach waiting to happen."],
ex:"A legacy control system runs an OS the vendor no longer patches. Rather than accept blind risk, the team isolates it on a segmented VLAN reachable only from a jump host, adds enhanced monitoring, and documents a risk-acceptance memo with a decommission date. When a wormable vulnerability for that OS is later published, the segmentation prevents reachability — the compensating controls held where patching could not.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-40 Rev.4","Guide to enterprise patch management planning — cadence, staging, and exceptions."],["CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog","Which vulnerabilities are actively exploited and warrant the fastest patch SLAs."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why is patch latency a first-order risk metric?",o:["Patches are expensive","Public vulnerabilities come with public exploits, and automated scanning finds unpatched exposed hosts within hours","Patching improves performance","It measures disk usage"],a:1,x:"The window between a fix's release and its deployment is exactly when exposed, unpatched systems are most reliably exploited."},
{q:"Staged patching (test, pilot ring, waves, rollback plan) exists to:",o:["Slow security down for no reason","Balance timely security against the availability risk of a bad update","Avoid ever patching","Replace backups"],a:1,x:"Staging catches update-induced breakage before it hits all of production while still closing the vulnerability on a defensible timeline."},
{q:"The correct treatment of a genuinely unpatchable legacy system is:",o:["Ignore it and hope","Apply compensating controls — isolation, monitoring — and document risk acceptance with an owner and review date","Connect it directly to the internet","Delete all its logs"],a:1,x:"Unpatchable does not mean undefendable; segmentation and monitoring plus documented, owned risk acceptance manage what patching cannot fix."}]},
{t:"Host-Based Firewalls and Endpoint Controls",d:"Local filtering, application control, and EDR.",
obj:["Explain the role of host-based firewalls alongside network firewalls","Describe application allowlisting and its strength","Explain what EDR adds beyond traditional antivirus"],
read:["Host-based firewalls (Windows Defender Firewall, Linux iptables/nftables/firewalld) filter traffic at the endpoint itself, providing defense that network segmentation cannot: they constrain what a host accepts and initiates even from other hosts on the same segment, limiting lateral movement between peers. In a well-designed environment, workstations block inbound connections from each other entirely — there is rarely a business reason for one user's laptop to reach another's over SMB or RDP, and blocking it at the host stops a whole class of lateral movement.",
"Application control (allowlisting) is one of the strongest endpoint controls available: instead of trying to block known-bad (antivirus's losing game), it permits only known-good, approved executables to run. Windows Defender Application Control / AppLocker and Linux equivalents can prevent unauthorized binaries — including most malware, which is by definition not on the allowlist — from executing at all. It is operationally demanding to maintain but blunts entire categories of attack, which is why security guidance ranks it among the highest-impact controls.",
"Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) extends beyond signature antivirus to behavioral monitoring, process lineage recording, and remote response. Where antivirus asks is this file known-bad, EDR asks is this behavior malicious — catching fileless attacks, living-off-the-land techniques, and novel malware by their actions (the parent-child anomalies and persistence patterns from module nine). EDR also gives responders the ability to isolate a host and investigate remotely, compressing containment time. Together, host firewall, application control, and EDR form the modern endpoint defense stack that the operational courses assume is present."],
ex:"Ransomware lands on a workstation via a malicious document. Application control blocks the unsigned payload from executing because it is not on the allowlist; even had it run, the host firewall blocks its attempt to spread to peer workstations over SMB, and EDR flags the document-spawns-PowerShell lineage. Three overlapping endpoint controls each independently break the attack chain.",
refs:[["Microsoft, Windows Defender Application Control","How allowlisting prevents unauthorized code execution."],["MITRE, EDR and behavioral detection concepts","How endpoint detection differs from signature antivirus."]],
kc:[
{q:"A host-based firewall provides protection that network segmentation cannot by:",o:["Encrypting the disk","Constraining traffic between peers on the same segment, blocking lateral movement between hosts","Replacing antivirus","Managing patches"],a:1,x:"Host firewalls filter at the endpoint, so even same-segment hosts can be prevented from reaching each other — limiting peer-to-peer lateral movement."},
{q:"Application allowlisting is powerful because it:",o:["Blocks only known malware signatures","Permits only approved executables to run, so unknown malware cannot execute at all","Speeds up the CPU","Requires no maintenance"],a:1,x:"By inverting the model to permit only known-good, allowlisting blocks novel malware by default since it is not on the approved list."},
{q:"EDR improves on traditional antivirus primarily by:",o:["Using more signatures","Detecting malicious behavior and process lineage, catching fileless and novel attacks, plus enabling remote response","Being cheaper","Disabling the firewall"],a:1,x:"EDR asks whether behavior is malicious rather than whether a file is known-bad, catching living-off-the-land techniques and enabling rapid containment."}]},
{t:"Disk Encryption, Secure Boot, and Firmware",d:"Protecting data at rest and the boot chain.",
obj:["Explain what full-disk encryption protects against and what it does not","Describe Secure Boot and measured boot and the role of the TPM","Recognize firmware and bootkit threats"],
read:["Full-disk encryption (BitLocker on Windows, LUKS on Linux) protects data at rest: if a device is lost, stolen, or its drive removed, the data is unreadable without the key. It is essential for laptops and mobile media, and it is the control that renders a stolen-but-encrypted device a possession loss rather than a confidentiality breach (the Parkerian distinction from BC-101). What it does not protect against is a running, unlocked system — once booted and authenticated, the data is decrypted for the OS, so disk encryption complements, not replaces, access control and endpoint defense.",
"The boot chain itself is a target. Bootkits and firmware implants load before the OS and its security tools, giving attackers persistence beneath the operating system that survives reimaging. Secure Boot counters this by verifying the cryptographic signature of each stage of the boot process, refusing to load unsigned or tampered bootloaders and kernels. Measured Boot goes further, recording hashes of each boot component into the Trusted Platform Module (TPM), enabling remote attestation — a server can verify that a machine booted a known-good chain before trusting it.",
"The TPM is the hardware root of trust that ties these together: it stores keys, seals disk-encryption keys to a known boot state (so the disk only unlocks if the boot chain is unmodified), and provides the attestation measurements. Modern endpoint security assumes these hardware features — virtualization-based security, credential protection, and disk encryption all lean on the TPM. As a defender you ensure they are enabled and monitored, because a machine with Secure Boot disabled or a TPM cleared unexpectedly has lost its foundational integrity guarantees."],
ex:"A laptop is stolen from a car. Because BitLocker sealed the key to the TPM and Secure Boot state, the thief cannot boot an alternate OS to bypass authentication nor read the drive externally — the loss is contained to hardware value, not data. Had the same laptop been left running and unlocked, encryption would have offered no protection, which is why endpoint controls layer on top.",
refs:[["Microsoft, BitLocker and TPM documentation","How disk encryption keys are sealed to boot state via the TPM."],["NIST SP 800-147 / 800-155","BIOS protection and firmware integrity measurement guidelines."]],
kc:[
{q:"Full-disk encryption primarily protects against:",o:["Malware on a running system","Data disclosure when a device or drive is lost, stolen, or accessed offline","Network attacks","Phishing"],a:1,x:"Encryption at rest protects offline/stolen media; once the system is booted and unlocked, the data is decrypted and other controls must protect it."},
{q:"Secure Boot defends against bootkits by:",o:["Encrypting the disk","Verifying the signature of each boot stage and refusing unsigned or tampered bootloaders/kernels","Blocking network traffic","Requiring a password at every boot"],a:1,x:"Signature verification of the boot chain prevents malicious code from loading beneath the OS, where it would evade OS-level defenses."},
{q:"The TPM strengthens disk encryption by:",o:["Making the disk faster","Sealing the encryption key to a known-good boot state so the disk only unlocks if the boot chain is unmodified","Storing user documents","Replacing the CPU"],a:1,x:"Binding the key to measured boot state means tampering with the boot chain prevents the disk from unlocking — hardware-rooted integrity."}]},
{t:"Endpoint Credential Protection",d:"Defending cached credentials, hashes, and tokens.",
obj:["Explain where credentials live in memory and on disk","Describe pass-the-hash and token-theft at a high level","Identify controls that protect endpoint credentials"],
read:["Endpoints store and cache credentials that attackers prize. On Windows, the LSASS process holds authentication material in memory — password hashes, Kerberos tickets, and tokens — and dumping LSASS is one of the most common post-compromise moves, because those credentials enable lateral movement to other systems without ever cracking a password. Cached domain credentials, saved browser passwords, and stored service-account secrets on disk are similarly targeted. Understanding that a single compromised endpoint can yield credentials that unlock the wider network reframes endpoint defense as network defense.",
"Credential-reuse attacks exploit this directly. Pass-the-hash uses a stolen password hash to authenticate without knowing the plaintext, and pass-the-ticket reuses Kerberos tickets similarly — both defeat the intuition that a strong, uncracked password is safe. Token theft (impersonating a logged-on user's token, connecting to earlier privilege modules) achieves the same lateral reach. These techniques are why a domain admin logging into an ordinary compromised workstation is so dangerous: their powerful credentials are now cached where the attacker can harvest them.",
"Defenses are concrete and layered: Credential Guard (virtualization-based isolation of LSASS secrets) prevents dumping, LSASS protection (RunAsPPL) hardens the process, and disciplined privileged-access practice — administrators use dedicated privileged workstations and never log powerful accounts into ordinary endpoints — shrinks where valuable credentials are exposed. Tiered administration models formalize this: high-privilege credentials are only ever used on high-trust systems. This module connects the endpoint to the identity attacks studied in depth in the operational tier and underpins the PAM and Zero Trust concepts of IAM."],
ex:"An attacker compromises a workstation and dumps LSASS, harvesting a cached domain-admin hash from an admin who had logged in to fix a problem the week before. Using pass-the-hash, they authenticate to the domain controller — full compromise from one workstation. Credential Guard would have protected the secrets, and a tiered-admin policy forbidding domain-admin logon to workstations would have prevented the exposure entirely.",
refs:[["Microsoft, Windows Defender Credential Guard","How virtualization-based security isolates LSASS secrets from theft."],["Microsoft, Securing privileged access (tiered model)","Why powerful credentials must never be used on lower-trust endpoints."]],
kc:[
{q:"Dumping the LSASS process on Windows lets an attacker obtain:",o:["Only the local user's documents","Cached credentials, hashes, and tickets usable for lateral movement to other systems","The BIOS password","Network diagrams"],a:1,x:"LSASS holds authentication material in memory; stealing it enables pass-the-hash/ticket lateral movement without cracking any password."},
{q:"Pass-the-hash is dangerous because it:",o:["Requires the plaintext password","Authenticates using a stolen hash directly, defeating the assumption that an uncracked strong password is safe","Only works offline","Needs physical access"],a:1,x:"The hash itself is sufficient to authenticate, so a strong password provides no protection once its hash is stolen from an endpoint."},
{q:"Why must domain-admin accounts never be used to log into ordinary workstations?",o:["It is slower","Their powerful credentials get cached where a workstation compromise can harvest them, enabling full-domain takeover","Workstations lack the software","It violates licensing"],a:1,x:"Logging privileged accounts into lower-trust systems exposes their credentials to theft; tiered administration confines them to high-trust hosts."}]},
{t:"CIS Benchmarks and Automated Auditing",d:"Turning hardening standards into measured compliance.",
obj:["Explain what CIS Benchmarks provide and how they are structured","Describe automated configuration auditing and continuous compliance","Explain remediation and documented exceptions"],
read:["CIS Benchmarks are consensus-developed, prescriptive configuration guides for operating systems and applications, structured as specific, testable recommendations (often with two levels — a broadly-applicable Level 1 and a more restrictive Level 2). They translate the abstract goal of hardening into a concrete, itemized standard: exactly which setting, exactly which value, and the rationale. This specificity is what makes hardening auditable — you can measure a system against the benchmark and get a percentage, not an opinion.",
"Automated auditing tools (CIS-CAT, OpenSCAP with SCAP content, DISA STIG scanners, and configuration-management platforms) assess a system against the benchmark and produce a scored report of pass/fail per item. This turns hardening from a one-time manual effort into continuous compliance: systems are scanned on a cadence, drift is detected, and the score trends over time — exactly the measurement discipline BC-101 argued for, applied to configuration. Configuration-as-code tools (Ansible, DSC) can both enforce and continuously reassert the hardened state, closing the drift loop.",
"No benchmark applies unmodified everywhere: some recommendations break required functionality, so the professional practice is to apply the benchmark, remediate what you can, and document justified exceptions as risk decisions with owners — not to silently skip items. A hardened environment is defined not by a perfect score but by a known, documented, monitored posture where every deviation from the standard has a reason on record. This auditability is what an assessor (BC-107) and an inspector (inspection readiness) actually look for."],
ex:"A team applies the CIS Level 1 benchmark to its server fleet and runs OpenSCAP weekly. The dashboard shows 94% compliance, with the 6% gap being documented exceptions (a legacy app requiring an older TLS setting, risk-accepted with a remediation date). When drift drops a server to 88%, the scan flags it and configuration-as-code reasserts the baseline — hardening became a measured, self-healing process.",
refs:[["CIS Benchmarks and CIS-CAT","The benchmark structure and the assessment tool that scores against it."],["NIST SP 800-126 (SCAP)","The Security Content Automation Protocol enabling automated configuration assessment."]],
kc:[
{q:"CIS Benchmarks make hardening auditable because they are:",o:["General principles open to interpretation","Specific, testable recommendations with defined settings and values, so compliance can be measured objectively","Antivirus signatures","Network diagrams"],a:1,x:"Their prescriptive, itemized form lets a tool score a system pass/fail per recommendation — turning hardening into a measurable percentage."},
{q:"Automated configuration auditing (e.g., OpenSCAP/CIS-CAT) primarily enables:",o:["One-time manual checks","Continuous compliance — scheduled scans that detect drift and trend the score over time","Faster networking","Disk encryption"],a:1,x:"Automated scanning converts hardening into ongoing measurement, surfacing configuration drift as it happens."},
{q:"The correct handling of a benchmark item that breaks required functionality is to:",o:["Silently skip it and move on","Document a justified exception as an owned, dated risk decision rather than an undocumented gap","Disable the whole benchmark","Ignore the functionality requirement"],a:1,x:"A defensible posture documents every deviation with a reason and owner; silent skips become invisible, unmanaged risk."}]},
{t:"Host Hardening Capstone Workflow",d:"Synthesizing OS defense into a repeatable, verifiable process.",
obj:["Sequence a complete host-hardening workflow end to end","Integrate configuration, logging, monitoring, and exceptions into one process","Verify hardening through auditing and simulated attack"],
read:["This module assembles the course into the workflow you will actually run. Start from a baseline (CIS/STIG/vendor) rather than a blank page; apply it via configuration management so it is uniform and self-healing (modules three, thirteen). Enforce least privilege on accounts and services and least functionality on what runs (modules two, six, seven, ten). Enable detection-grade logging — Windows audit policy plus Sysmon, or Linux auditd — and forward it centrally so it survives tampering (modules four, nine). Layer endpoint controls: host firewall, application control, EDR (module twelve). Manage patches on severity-based SLAs with compensating controls for the unpatchable (module eleven).",
"The sequence matters because the steps depend on each other: logging without central forwarding is erasable; application control without a baseline of legitimate software is unmanageable; patching without inventory is guesswork. A coherent workflow — inventory, baseline, harden, instrument, monitor, patch, document — produces a host that is not merely configured once but continuously maintained in a known-good state, with every exception recorded. This is the operational reality behind the abstract phrase defense in depth on the endpoint.",
"Verification closes the loop. Hardening you cannot measure you cannot trust, so the workflow ends by auditing against the benchmark (a compliance score), reviewing logs to confirm the expected events are actually being captured, and ideally validating with simulated attacker techniques (does the host log and block a test malicious PowerShell? does application control stop an unapproved binary?). The graded deliverable for this course is exactly this: a hardened Windows and Linux host with a compliance report, a logging validation, and a documented exception list — the whole course expressed as a verifiable artifact."],
ex:"A team hardens a new Ubuntu server: applies the CIS benchmark via Ansible, disables password SSH and unnecessary services, configures auditd with forwarding to the SIEM, enables the host firewall and unattended security updates, then verifies by running an OpenSCAP scan (96%, exceptions documented) and a test that a malicious command triggers an auditd event in the SIEM. The host is provably hardened, not just assumed to be — the difference this course exists to teach.",
refs:[["DISA STIG and SCAP content","Defense-grade hardening plus the automated verification content."],["NIST SP 800-123","Guide to general server security — the end-to-end hardening lifecycle."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why must central log forwarding be part of the hardening workflow, not an afterthought?",o:["It speeds up the host","Because local logs are attacker-erasable; forwarding preserves evidence and enables cross-host correlation","It replaces the firewall","Logs are otherwise illegal"],a:1,x:"Hardening that captures logs but leaves them locally erasable fails at the moment of compromise; forwarding makes the telemetry tamper-resistant."},
{q:"Application control depends on which prior step to be manageable?",o:["Disabling logging","A baseline inventory of legitimate, approved software to allowlist","Removing the host firewall","Public internet exposure"],a:1,x:"Allowlisting requires knowing what should be allowed; without a software baseline, maintaining the allowlist is impossible."},
{q:"The hardening workflow ends with verification because:",o:["It is a formality","Hardening that is not measured (benchmark scan, log validation, simulated attack) cannot be trusted or defended to an assessor","Verification is faster than hardening","It replaces documentation"],a:1,x:"Measured verification turns assumed security into demonstrated security — the auditable posture assessors and inspectors require."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A service account holds SeBackupPrivilege via Backup Operators membership and is phished. The attacker reads NTDS.dit. The root cause is:",o:["A zero-day in Windows","Over-privileged group membership granting a system-wide privilege that bypasses file permissions","Weak encryption","A firewall misconfiguration"],a:1,x:"SeBackupPrivilege overrides DACLs; the vulnerability was excessive privileged-group membership, not a software flaw — least privilege would have prevented it."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A developer has sudo NOPASSWD access to vi. After phishing, the attacker runs sudo vi and escapes to a root shell. The lesson is:",o:["vi is malware","Sudo access to shell-capable programs equals full root; grants must be scoped to non-escaping, specific commands","Passwords are unnecessary","The firewall failed"],a:1,x:"Programs that spawn subshells convert narrow sudo grants into full root — a GTFOBins-class escalation from a single generous sudoers line."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A host's local Security log is short; Event ID 1102 is present. Investigation still succeeds because:",o:["The attacker left a note","Windows Event Forwarding shipped the events centrally before the local clear, and the 1102 itself is a flag","Logs cannot be cleared","The firewall logged everything"],a:1,x:"Central collection defeats local log-clearing, and the clear event (1102) is itself high-signal anti-forensics evidence."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An SMBv1 ransomware worm sweeps an industry. Immune organizations had:",o:["Faster internet","Disabled SMBv1 via Group Policy, removing the network-propagation vector","More antivirus licenses","Larger disks"],a:1,x:"A single centrally-enforced hardening setting closed the legacy protocol the worm used to spread machine to machine."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An incident responder finds a setuid-root copy of a shell at /usr/local/bin/.sh. This is:",o:["A normal system binary","An attacker backdoor — any user can spawn a root shell — found only by auditing special permission bits","A compiler artifact","A logging tool"],a:1,x:"Standard permissions look normal; the setuid-root bit on a shell is a privilege-escalation backdoor that only special-bit auditing reveals."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A Linux web server is compromised and /var/log/auth.log is truncated, but the full attacker command sequence is still recovered. How?",o:["From the DHCP server","auditd execve logging was configured and forwarded centrally before truncation","From the browser cache","From the BIOS"],a:1,x:"Deliberately configured auditd captured every command and forwarding preserved it beyond the attacker's local truncation."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Ransomware runs but cannot spread to peer workstations or execute its main payload. The most likely reasons are:",o:["Luck","Application control blocked the unapproved binary and the host firewall blocked peer SMB — overlapping endpoint controls","The network was down","The attacker gave up"],a:1,x:"Allowlisting stops unknown executables and host firewalls stop peer lateral movement — each independently breaks the chain."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A legacy OS cannot be patched for a newly published wormable bug, yet the host is not compromised. The defense was:",o:["Ignoring the vulnerability","Compensating controls — segmentation to a jump-host-only VLAN plus monitoring — with documented risk acceptance","Deleting the host's logs","Connecting it to the internet for updates"],a:1,x:"When patching is impossible, isolation and monitoring plus owned, dated risk acceptance manage the risk the patch would have removed."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A new systemd service named 'sysupdate' runs a binary from /tmp on a hardened server. The strongest indicator that this is malicious is:",o:["It uses systemd","It deviates from the captured baseline of legitimate services and runs from an unusual path","It has a lowercase name","It uses TCP"],a:1,x:"Deviation from a known-good service inventory plus an unusual binary path is a classic persistence indicator, independent of any signature."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A hardening effort is declared complete. What proves it rather than merely asserting it?",o:["A signed memo that it is done","An automated benchmark compliance score, log-capture validation, and a simulated-attack test, with documented exceptions","The absence of alerts","A vendor's marketing sheet"],a:1,x:"Measured verification — compliance scan, logging validation, and attack simulation — turns assumed hardening into demonstrated, auditable hardening."}
]};
